A Bare-Metal Kernel
====================

A minimal, educational kernel written in C and assembly.  Boots directly
in M-mode on QEMU with no BIOS, bootloader, or runtime.  Supports
user-mode processes, a virtual filesystem, a shell, and multiple programs.

Quick Start
-----------

```bash
# 1.  Build kernel + user programs
make ARCH=riscv        # default, also works: ARCH=arm
cd usr && make         # build hello, init, sh, cat

# 2.  Create disk image
python3 tools/mkdisk.py disk.img \
    usr/init.bin  /bin/init \
    usr/sh.bin    /bin/sh   \
    usr/hello.bin /bin/hello

# 3.  Run
qemu-system-riscv64 -machine virt -bios none -kernel kernel.elf \
    -nographic -smp 2 \
    -drive file=disk.img,format=raw,if=none,id=blk \
    -device virtio-blk-device,drive=blk
```

What Happens
------------

```text
QEMU loads kernel at 0x80000000
  → hart 0 wins atomic lottery, calls kmain()
    → traps, timers, memory allocator
    → loads user binaries from disk into ramfs
    → spawns init (PID 0, U-mode)
      → init spawns /bin/sh
        → sh prints "$ ", reads commands, spawns programs
```

Type `hello` in the shell to run the demo.  Type `x` to exit (init will
re-spawn a new shell).

Building Your Own Programs
--------------------------

User programs are plain C, compiled as position-independent flat binaries:

```c
// myapp.c
#include "usr.h"   // write, read, spawn, wait, exit, yield, memcpy, memset

void main(void) {
    write(1, "Hello!\n", 7);
    exit(0);
}
```

```bash
riscv64-elf-gcc -nostdlib -ffreestanding -fno-builtin -Os \
    -Wl,-Ttext=0 -o myapp.elf crt0.S myapp.c
riscv64-elf-objcopy -O binary myapp.elf myapp.bin
```

Then add it to the disk image:

```bash
python3 tools/mkdisk.py disk.img \
    usr/init.bin  /bin/init \
    usr/sh.bin    /bin/sh   \
    myapp.bin     /bin/myapp
```

Type `myapp` in the shell to run it.

The disk format uses sector 0 for a file table (magic 0x52414D46), with
binary data in subsequent sectors.  At boot the kernel loads all files
from the block device into ramfs.  After boot, the block device is
available for I/O but the files are served from memory.

Custom Init
-----------

The kernel tries init paths in order and runs the first one found:

```text
/bin/init   (default)
/bin/sh     (shell fallback)
/bin/hello  (smoke test)
```

Override at build time:

```bash
make CFLAGS='-DINIT_PATH=\"/sbin/myinit\"'
```

Or just name your init `/bin/init` on the disk image.

Syscalls
--------

| nr | name    | args             | returns  | description               |
|----|---------|------------------|----------|---------------------------|
| 1  | write   | fd, buf, len     | bytes    | write to fd (1 = stdout)  |
| 2  | exit    | code             | —        | terminate process         |
| 3  | yield   | —                | —        | give up CPU voluntarily   |
| 7  | read    | fd, buf, len     | bytes    | read from fd (0 = stdin)  |
| 8  | spawn   | path             | child    | load & run program        |
| 9  | wait    | pid              | child    | reap exited child         |

Debugging with GDB
------------------

```bash
# Terminal 1: start QEMU with GDB stub
qemu-system-riscv64 -machine virt -bios none -kernel kernel.elf -s -S &

# Terminal 2: attach GDB
riscv64-elf-gdb kernel.elf -ex "target remote localhost:1234"

# Common commands:
#   break kmain       — break at kernel entry
#   break trap_handler — break on any trap
#   info registers    — dump all registers
```

Build Options
-------------

```bash
make ARCH=riscv                       # default (RISCV 64)
make ARCH=arm CROSS_COMPILE=arm-none-eabi-  # ARM
make ENABLE_DEBUG=1                        # debug symbols
make SMP=4                                 # 4 simulated harts
```

Directory Layout
----------------

```text
Makefile              # top-level build (kbuild-style)
arch/riscv/           # boot, drivers, trap handling (asm + C)
include/              # kernel headers (types.h, proc.h, fs.h, ...)
include/arch/riscv/   # RISC-V ISA headers (csr.h, mmu.h, trap.h, ...)
sys/                  # kernel subsystems + utilities (pmm, proc, fs, ...)
usr/                  # user programs (crt0, usr.h, hello, init, sh, cat)
tools/mkdisk.py       # disk image builder
scripts/              # build system internals
```

Requirements
------------

- RISC-V: `riscv64-elf-gcc`, `qemu-system-riscv64`
- ARM (WIP): `arm-none-eabi-gcc`, `qemu-system-arm`
- Python 3 (for disk image builder)
